<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Amoghavarsha I</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 800–<a href="/years/878">878</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/9th-century">9th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Amoghavarsha I was the longest-reigning Rashtrakuta emperor, ruling the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/deccan">Deccan</a> from 814 to 878. He founded the capital <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manyakheta">Manyakheta</a>, was a patron of Jain teachers such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jinasena">Jinasena</a>, and is credited with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kavirajamarga">Kavirajamarga</a>, the earliest surviving work on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kannada">Kannada</a> poetics, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanskrit">Sanskrit</a> Prashnottara Ratnamalika. His long reign was marked more by cultural flowering than by conquest.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/amoghavarsha-i.jpg" alt="Amoghavarsha I" width="320" height="551" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: B. Lewis Rice (Benjamin Lewis Rice, 1837-1927) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Old_Kannada_inscription_(876_AD)_of_Rashtrakuta_King_Amoghavarsha_I_at_Veerabhadra_temple_in_Kumsi.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
